WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.239
It's great to welcome to the program today.

00:00:02.239 --> 00:00:07.689
Ian Haney Lopez, who is a constitutional law
professor at the University of California,

00:00:07.689 --> 00:00:13.150
Berkeley and also author of the book Merge
Left fuzing Race and Class Winning Elections

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:14.490
and Saving America.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.860
It's so great to have you on today.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:17.860
Thank you.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:18.860
It's really great to join you.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:25.560
So the angle I'm very interested in is that
of really thinking about how to win, because

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:31.800
one of the areas where I've been a bit disaffected
lately with my own movement, the progressive

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:38.239
movement in which, you know, I consider myself
a member, is that sometimes I think we may

00:00:38.239 --> 00:00:42.460
miss the forest for the trees when it comes
to how to win.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:50.219
And you talk very interestingly about the
right and wrong way to deal with race in politics.

00:00:50.219 --> 00:00:55.769
And sometimes the arguments that are made
are I feel overly simplistic, like identity

00:00:55.769 --> 00:01:01.710
politics is bad or good versus actually a
discussion of what we mean by that and how

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:05.070
something is important to identity as race
should be discussed.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:11.300
So maybe to start lay out the panorama, as
you see it, of how some of these issues are

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:15.570
being discussed in politics and how you would
maybe want to see that changed.

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:22.250
So I think the number one thing to start with
there is the idea that race is being used

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:29.000
as a wedge issue to divide Americans and more
particularly to divide a Democratic constituency

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.950
that used to include the white working class
African-Americans and white liberals.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:41.760
Everybody understands that race is being used
as a wedge issue, but the left today is badly

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.730
divided in how to respond.

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:49.870
The more centrist position, though, this is
a position that's also adopted by economic

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:56.790
populists like Bernie Sanders, is to focus
on winning over white voters and to understand

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:00.900
or to believe that talking about race will
alienate white voters.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:08.060
And so to adopt as a racial strategy the tactic
of not talking about race at all.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:14.049
So all of this sort of among centrist Democrats
and among some sort of radical economic populist,

00:02:14.049 --> 00:02:19.700
there's a tendency to go colorblind, to emphasize
economic issues and to downplay the centrality

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:20.700
of race.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:21.870
That's on one side.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:26.700
On the other side, especially among racial
justice activists, you have a very strong

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:30.569
sense that we need to talk about race.

00:02:30.569 --> 00:02:35.239
We need to talk about it both in terms of
the systematic violence by government against

00:02:35.239 --> 00:02:41.389
communities of color, violence that's evident
in mass incarceration and over policing and

00:02:41.389 --> 00:02:47.280
mass deportation, ice enforcement, but also
in the consistent neglect of communities of

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.730
color that we need to address it and we need
to address as well frontally and head on the

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:56.870
use of race as a as a wedge issue in politics.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:59.690
That's the that's the divide.

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:03.590
Both camps are thinking about how to deal
with race.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:05.670
And they're coming up with very different
solutions.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:12.170
Ignore it and hope you can change the conversation
or attack it head on.

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:19.099
So there is one I'll call it a proposed solution
that I read about frequently.

00:03:19.099 --> 00:03:24.069
And I want to see whether you think that it
really is sort of up to the challenge of dealing

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:30.389
with this issue in a productive way, which
is talk about race, but always make it a race

00:03:30.389 --> 00:03:33.279
class narrative like that's that's the prescription.

00:03:33.279 --> 00:03:34.279
That's how you do it.

00:03:34.279 --> 00:03:37.640
You talk about class understanding that race
is part of it.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:38.779
You meld them together.

00:03:38.779 --> 00:03:40.159
You don't alienate anybody.

00:03:40.159 --> 00:03:41.480
Does does that shake?

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:43.909
How do you how would you analyze that idea?

00:03:43.909 --> 00:03:49.870
So so that's the idea that I sort of come
to a number of years ago and worked on with

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:55.319
unions and then ultimately put together a
research project, the race class narrative

00:03:55.319 --> 00:04:02.040
project, using communication specialists and
pollsters.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:03.340
And here's the core idea.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:07.510
And I and I really want to make this point
as sharply as I can.

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:11.450
This is not a typical race plus class.

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:14.959
We should do both type move.

00:04:14.959 --> 00:04:21.389
This is a paradigm change in how we think
about the relationship between race and class.

00:04:21.389 --> 00:04:27.620
It's a paradigm change because what it's saying
is race and class are not in the United States

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:28.630
separate.

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:31.449
They're completely dependent on each other.

00:04:31.449 --> 00:04:38.560
Or to put that more concretely in the class
war that the rich are winning, their number

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.699
one weapon is racial division.

00:04:41.699 --> 00:04:48.900
The rich are siphoning more and more wealth
to themselves because they've been successful

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.479
in dividing us along race lines.

00:04:51.479 --> 00:04:54.669
And this has very clear implications if you.

00:04:54.669 --> 00:04:57.729
Want to if you want economic populism.

00:04:57.729 --> 00:05:02.669
And if you want racial justice, because what
it says is if you want economic populism,

00:05:02.669 --> 00:05:08.960
you have to fight race as a do as a tactic
for dividing American voters.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:13.430
And what it says is if you want racial justice,
you have to fight racial division.

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:19.610
You have to make it clear to whites that they
have their own pragmatic interests in opposing

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:24.479
racial division so that whites will understand
that they have their own pragmatic interest

00:05:24.479 --> 00:05:26.789
in supporting racial justice.

00:05:26.789 --> 00:05:35.349
I'm reminded of the sort of status quo that
exists in much of the agricultural world that

00:05:35.349 --> 00:05:42.240
deals with immigration, where we've seen large
corporations that benefit from both documented

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:50.009
and undocumented immigrant labor very successfully
convince a lot of Americans that this is really

00:05:50.009 --> 00:05:53.889
about our brown people stealing your jobs.

00:05:53.889 --> 00:06:00.550
And that seems to me to have been a very effective
way of taking the attention off of the fact

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:04.250
that these corporations want to pay the lowest
possible wages, even if they are not fair

00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:10.490
wages, that these corporations disproportionately
take advantage with very little consequence

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:15.189
of undocumented immigrant labor because those
rules are often ignored by the government

00:06:15.189 --> 00:06:16.250
as priorities.

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:22.080
Is that an example of how this is being weaponized
in a sense, as you describe?

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:27.860
You know, it's a really great example because
it illustrates two distinct phenomena.

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:30.780
We have to keep both in mind.

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:35.630
One way to think about the connections between
race and class is to think about the way in

00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:42.830
which economic exploitation of people of color
has been the foundation for this country.

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:48.530
So you can think about it as the racialization
of Native Americans, as inferior as an excuse

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:54.210
to take land or the racialization of people
from Africa as black, as an excuse for slavery

00:06:54.210 --> 00:07:00.389
and so on through Manifest Destiny and through
today's exploitation of immigrant labor and

00:07:00.389 --> 00:07:03.020
especially undocumented immigrant labor.

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:09.490
This is a story that says race and class focus
on what's happening to communities of color

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:11.060
in the way they're economically exploited.

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:13.629
A very, very important story.

00:07:13.629 --> 00:07:15.700
But here's the point.

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:17.979
Don't stop there.

00:07:17.979 --> 00:07:22.759
The race and class are fuzed throughout the
American economy.

00:07:22.759 --> 00:07:27.889
And as importantly, throughout the American
government, which regulates the economy.

00:07:27.889 --> 00:07:31.210
And this is the other part of the story that
you surfaced.

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:37.979
Not only are corporations explored exploiting
brown and black and Asian people, but they're

00:07:37.979 --> 00:07:45.139
also turning around and saying to whites the
cause of economic misery in your life or these

00:07:45.139 --> 00:07:51.900
undeserving threatening others, blame them,
hate government, because government supposedly

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:55.669
cares more about them than it does about you.

00:07:55.669 --> 00:08:03.849
You're on your own and a buy a gun and B,
trust the marketplace because through hard

00:08:03.849 --> 00:08:05.560
work you'll take care of yourself.

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:09.330
And if you fail, you alone are responsible.

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:16.090
And what this means is that the entire economy,
the entire government, the way government

00:08:16.090 --> 00:08:22.830
is captured by the rich is also a function
of racial narratives in our political sphere,

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:23.849
in our culture.

00:08:23.849 --> 00:08:25.949
This is incredibly important.

00:08:25.949 --> 00:08:29.729
Race and class is not just a story about what's
happening to communities of color.

00:08:29.729 --> 00:08:33.099
It's a story about what's happening to the
United States as a whole.

00:08:33.099 --> 00:08:37.870
It's a story about what has happened to us
over the last 50 years.

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:44.070
We've seen the greatest transfer of wealth
upward from all of us into the economic stratosphere

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:47.160
that we've seen in a century.

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:48.160
Why?

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:51.330
Because many Americans were convinced to vote
their racial fears.

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:59.120
You cannot understand surging economic inequality
that impoverishes almost all of us, including

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:03.440
whites, without focusing on the way in which
race has been used as the principal weapon

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:04.940
against all of us.

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:05.940
Yes.

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:07.660
So that gets to Trump in 2016.

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:13.370
And I would be interested in hearing your
thoughts about how in the post-mortems of

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:19.360
what happened, there have been all sorts of
different analysis that purport to know what

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:21.880
it was that allowed Donald Trump to win.

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:27.200
Now, one merely mathematical point is when
you're talking about about seventy thousand

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:29.490
votes in three states, that could have flipped
it.

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:34.430
We want to be careful about pointing to any
one thing, because there's probably 30 different

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:37.530
things that could have made a seventy thousand
vote difference.

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:44.200
But what I want to focus it on with you is
the fear of losing status.

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:49.360
That was in the minds of many voters who did
ultimately vote for Trump.

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:58.540
How does that tie into the race class analysis?
Trump wins overwhelmingly because of his success

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:03.160
in appealing to racial resentment and racial
anxiety.

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:10.540
But we should be clear, Trump was not the
originator of that strategy.

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:13.029
That's a 50 year old strategy.

00:10:13.029 --> 00:10:15.310
Republicans have been trying that since 1964.

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:16.310
Right.

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:22.399
And especially since 1970, it has been their
principal route to building popular support

00:10:22.399 --> 00:10:24.500
to winning elections.

00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:30.029
So when we look at Mitt Romney's numbers of
90 percent of Mitt Romney's support came from

00:10:30.029 --> 00:10:31.290
white voters.

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:33.460
Same with with Donald Trump.

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:35.889
Three out of five whites voted for Mitt Romney.

00:10:35.889 --> 00:10:37.490
Same with Donald Trump.

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:41.220
98 percent of Republican elected officials
are white.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:43.490
Similar numbers under Trump.

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:50.730
Donald Trump pushes more aggressively a strategy
that has been in place and has been working

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:53.509
for the Republicans for 50 years.

00:10:53.509 --> 00:10:59.850
Not since 1964 has a Democratic candidate
for president won a majority of the white

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:00.850
vote.

00:11:00.850 --> 00:11:04.490
This is very, very much about race.

00:11:04.490 --> 00:11:10.079
But it's also important to pause and say that
doesn't mean this isn't about class.

00:11:10.079 --> 00:11:11.690
There's Arnold.

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:12.690
Is that this debate?

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:15.050
Is it race or is it class?

00:11:15.050 --> 00:11:19.990
For many people afraid of losing status, they
have lost status.

00:11:19.990 --> 00:11:21.980
They have lost position.

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:23.700
Their lives are in peril.

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:25.160
Many whites are in trouble.

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:27.320
They're under tremendous stress.

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:34.260
This is the sort of research that talks now
about depths of despair, suicide, violence,

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:39.019
drug addiction, like most of us.

00:11:39.019 --> 00:11:41.649
Many whites are under tremendous pressure.

00:11:41.649 --> 00:11:46.829
And what's happening is that the Republicans
have created that pressure by siphoning wealth

00:11:46.829 --> 00:11:49.600
up into the up to the very rich.

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:54.770
But they've also turned around and said, you
know, why in your life is in such danger?

00:11:54.770 --> 00:11:57.029
It's because of people of color.

00:11:57.029 --> 00:11:58.029
They're coming in.

00:11:58.029 --> 00:11:59.279
They're flooding the country.

00:11:59.279 --> 00:12:01.399
They're taking what they don't deserve.

00:12:01.399 --> 00:12:02.720
And they're also violent.

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:04.830
And they don't respect life the way with the.

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:05.830
We do, right.

00:12:05.830 --> 00:12:11.000
This is a race story to provide a frame to
interpret economic hardship.

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:14.510
I want to be very clear.

00:12:14.510 --> 00:12:17.940
Economic hardship is not the main thing here.

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:23.269
Many people like Robert Reisz start by saying
economic hardship verte.

00:12:23.269 --> 00:12:26.519
First, then people look for someone to blame.

00:12:26.519 --> 00:12:28.279
That's not the history.

00:12:28.279 --> 00:12:32.120
Dog whistling starts and is tremendously successful
at a time.

00:12:32.120 --> 00:12:38.730
In 1970 1972, when most working class whites
were doing remarkably well.

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:46.149
Race has its own independent power, but race
and class combined in a vicious circle.

00:12:46.149 --> 00:12:53.209
The more people vote, the racial fears, the
more they elect politicians who serve the

00:12:53.209 --> 00:12:57.950
plutocrats, the more those politicians turn
around and rigged the system to help very

00:12:57.950 --> 00:13:00.240
rich and beggar the rest of us.

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:06.360
The more they feel stressed and look for someone
to blame and are encouraged to use race as

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:11.470
the focus of their hatred and their anxiety,
it becomes a vicious circle and no one better

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:14.360
embodies that than Donald Trump.

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:18.029
The last thing I want to touch on and we have
sort of limited time left is I want to get

00:13:18.029 --> 00:13:23.680
your thoughts on the identity politics debate
that is sort of existing on the left.

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:31.120
I'll give you my sort of 20 second position,
which is I am against the use of identity

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:37.459
as a cudgel, as a basis on which to silence
someone, as the basis on which to disqualify

00:13:37.459 --> 00:13:39.480
somebody, etc..

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:47.120
But I do believe that one's identity gives
insight into what it is like to experience

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:49.050
the world through that identity.

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:51.130
And so we should want that.

00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:53.330
We should welcome that and we should listen
to it.

00:13:53.330 --> 00:13:55.329
That's where I kind of land on it.

00:13:55.329 --> 00:13:59.420
I don't know if that is a coherent strategy
for electoral politics, however.

00:13:59.420 --> 00:14:00.870
Oh, I think it is.

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:07.459
I would just extend it further and I would
say it's always identity politics more than

00:14:07.459 --> 00:14:08.709
anything else.

00:14:08.709 --> 00:14:13.269
Political scientists and Democratic realists
understand people are voting in terms of a

00:14:13.269 --> 00:14:17.040
series of questions who am I in this society?

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:18.449
Who are my friends?

00:14:18.449 --> 00:14:19.700
Who threatens me?

00:14:19.700 --> 00:14:21.829
Who can I work with?

00:14:21.829 --> 00:14:25.459
Those are questions of identity and we're
all thinking in those terms.

00:14:25.459 --> 00:14:30.500
So the problem with a term like identity politics
is when it's applied to some groups, but not

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:31.760
to others.

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:37.690
We should all be thinking that all of us are
operating on organizing, voting in terms of

00:14:37.690 --> 00:14:38.690
identity.

00:14:38.690 --> 00:14:45.670
And what that means is we need to create this
sort of identity that includes an awful lot

00:14:45.670 --> 00:14:51.589
of us, a super majority of us, because only
with a bigger we only with an inclusive identity

00:14:51.589 --> 00:14:53.520
that takes.

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:54.520
Seriously?

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:59.699
Can we actually create the wave election that
we need to change the direction in this country

00:14:59.699 --> 00:15:06.160
and this is where that race class fusion becomes
so important, because what it says is all

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:08.060
of us want to take care of our families.

00:15:08.060 --> 00:15:12.839
All of us want to a world in which we're welcome
and respected and safe.

00:15:12.839 --> 00:15:15.230
But some people are trying to divide us.

00:15:15.230 --> 00:15:17.970
Let's come together over those lines of division.

00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:22.029
Let's come together specifically across racial
lines.

00:15:22.029 --> 00:15:26.839
Because when we come together and understand
we're all in this together, then and only

00:15:26.839 --> 00:15:31.020
then can we build the political power to really
change this country's direction.

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:36.430
And that's going to be good for all our families,
whether we're white, black or brown.

00:15:36.430 --> 00:15:40.980
Everybody's helped when we realize all of
our fates are linked.

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:42.129
Very, very interesting.

00:15:42.129 --> 00:15:46.699
We've been speaking with Ian Haney Lopez,
who is a constitutional law professor.

00:15:46.699 --> 00:15:51.839
The book Merge Left fuzing Race and Class
Winning Elections and Saving America.

00:15:51.839 --> 00:15:54.089
Thank you so much for talking to me today.

00:15:54.089 --> 00:15:56.100
I really appreciate your time.

00:15:56.100 --> 00:15:57.100
Thanks so much.

00:15:57.100 --> 00:15:58.339
I really appreciate the conversation.

